# /etc/pam.d/hyprlock-fido2 — lean auth stack for the hyprlock screen lock.
#
# WHY a separate service name instead of editing /etc/pam.d/hyprlock: that file is
# owned by the hyprlock RPM, while hyprlock reads whatever service
# `auth:pam:module` names (src/auth/Pam.cpp). An own filename cannot be clobbered
# by a package update — and if this file ever goes missing, hyprlock falls back to
# /etc/pam.d/su, i.e. degraded to the old behaviour, never a lockout.
#
# WHY not the shipped `auth include login`: `login` pulls in system-auth, whose
# authselect **sssd** profile appends, after pam_unix:
#
#     auth  sufficient  pam_sss.so forward_pass
#
# There is no SSSD domain on this machine, so that module only ever prompts for
# the password a second time and fails. That is the "the FIDO stick is not active
# until I hit Enter three times" symptom (diagnosed 2026-08-06):
#
#   1. locking starts a PAM transaction; pam_u2f arms the key immediately
#   2. ~31s later the CTAP device timeout lapses and the u2f leg is spent; PAM
#      falls through to pam_unix's "Password:" — the key is now dead, but the lock
#      screen still says "touch key"
#   3. returning minutes later, Enter #1 fails pam_unix, Enter #2 fails pam_sss,
#      pam_deny ends the transaction, and only then does hyprlock's retry loop
#      (Pam.cpp:86-105) start a fresh transaction that re-arms the key — so the
#      touch is the third interaction
#
# hyprlock's own duplicate-prompt suppression does not help: the repeated prompt
# arrives in a separate conv() call, where its `initialPrompt` local is true again
# (Pam.cpp:20,32). Dropping pam_sss makes one Enter enough. This also drops
# `pam_faildelay delay=2000000` — a 2s penalty per failed attempt that a lock
# screen has no use for — plus pam_env/pam_nologin/pam_securetty/pam_selinux,
# none of which mean anything for unlocking an existing session.
#
# The ~31s window itself is NOT fixable here: it is a CTAP timeout in the
# authenticator, and pam_u2f exposes no timeout option.
#
# ORDER: pam_u2f first, so a bare touch unlocks and the key is armed the instant
# the screen locks. pam_unix is `required` rather than `sufficient` so this stack
# cannot silently pass, and carries no `nullok` — an empty submission must fail
# (that failure is what re-arms the key).
#
# Nothing is given up relative to the login stack: there is no pam_faillock
# anywhere in this system's PAM config, and hyprlock only ever calls pam_start /
# pam_authenticate / pam_end — no account, session or credential phase. The
# `account` line below is purely defensive, in case a future hyprlock adds
# pam_acct_mgmt.
#
# pam_u2f's own options live in /etc/security/pam_u2f.conf (userverification = 1);
# see bootstrap/pam/pam_u2f.conf. Options on a module line would override that
# file, so deliberately none are set here beyond `cue`.
#
# Install via:  mise run bootstrap:pam-hyprlock
# Selected by:  auth { pam { module = hyprlock-fido2 } }  in hyprlock.conf
# See ~/Documents/docs/fido2-login-latency-power-management.md

auth        sufficient  pam_u2f.so cue
auth        required    pam_unix.so

account     required    pam_unix.so
